# game/ch3_governance.rpy:10
translate plain ch3_governance_35dd89a6:

    # j "Turnout on the last three proposals: six point three percent. I'm answering four hundred DMs a day and the megaphone guy has opinions about the logo."
    j "Turnout on the last three proposals: six point three percent. That's how many members actually voted."
    j "I'm answering four hundred DMs a day. And the megaphone guy has opinions about our logo."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:11
translate plain ch3_governance_8a655085:

    # narrator "Democracy, it turns out, is a chore. Chores get skipped."
    narrator "It turns out democracy is a chore, like washing dishes. And people skip chores."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:16
translate plain ch3_governance_e4783963:

    # m "Proposal #17 just went up: 'Redistribute founder tokens to the community.' Thirty-five percent support and climbing."
    m "Proposal #17 just went up. It says: 'Redistribute founder tokens to the community.'"
    m "That means: take tokens from us founders and give them to members. Thirty-five percent support it, and that number is growing."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:17
translate plain ch3_governance_81a95805:

    # m "Here's the uncomfortable part: half their arguments are good."
    m "Here's the uncomfortable part. Half of their arguments are good."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} Rallying votes means you campaign, then let members decide in an open vote. It is fair, but the result is not certain."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} A veto is a special founder power to cancel a decision alone. It is fast and certain, but it shows the founders still hold the real power."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:20
translate plain ch3_governance_2c4d8cd5:

    # v "Before you touch that veto, founder: I watched a hundred union locals win that exact battle and lose everything after. There's a third path if you're brave enough to look weak."
    v "Before you touch that veto, founder, listen to me."
    v "I watched a hundred local union groups win this exact fight. Then they lost everything afterwards."
    v "There's a third path. But you have to be brave enough to look weak."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} The compromise is a founder burn. The founders destroy 10 percent of their own tokens on purpose. You give up some tokens and votes by choice."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:26
translate plain ch3_governance_a269abe0:

    # narrator "Vote: 48 for, 47 against, 5 abstain. You win by a coin flip's width. Factions crystallize overnight."
    narrator "The vote: 48 for, 47 against, 5 abstain. You win by the smallest possible margin."
    narrator "By the next morning, the community has split into clear, opposing groups."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:31
translate plain ch3_governance_d6e6a898:

    # narrator "The proposal dies instantly. So does something less visible. 'Decentralized*' — the asterisk is yours now."
    narrator "The proposal dies instantly. Something less visible dies too."
    narrator "Now people write your project's name with a star: 'Decentralized*'. The star means 'not really'. And that star belongs to you now."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:35
translate plain ch3_governance_3998f01c:

    # v "Losing a little on purpose so you can't lose everything by force. That's the oldest trick organized people have."
    v "You lose a little on purpose. Then nobody can take everything from you by force."
    v "Organized people have used that trick for a very long time."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:39
translate plain ch3_governance_e5c88cc1:

    # narrator "Turnout keeps falling. Someone proposes delegation — 'liquid democracy.' Someone else livestreams a pie chart: in comparable DAOs, the top 10%% of voters hold 76.2%% of the power."
    narrator "Turnout keeps falling. Someone suggests delegation, also called 'liquid democracy'. You give your vote to someone you trust, and they vote for you."
    narrator "Someone else livestreams a pie chart. In similar DAOs, the top 10 percent of voters hold 76.2 percent of the power."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} Open delegation lets anyone give their vote to anyone. More people take part, but power can collect in a few hands."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} Capped, rotating delegation limits each delegate's power and changes delegates over time. Time-locks add a waiting period before big proposals take effect. This is safer, but slower. Tabling it means you decide later."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:44
translate plain ch3_governance_88e347c8:

    # narrator "Turnout doubles. So does the influence of exactly eleven people."
    narrator "Turnout doubles. But so does the power of exactly eleven people."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:50
translate plain ch3_governance_66ad2bcd:

    # narrator "Clunky. Slower. Attack-resistant. Maya calls it 'beautifully boring.'"
    narrator "It's clunky and slower. But it is hard to attack. Maya calls it 'beautifully boring.'"

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:55
translate plain ch3_governance_82fa52ae:

    # narrator "The governance forum quietly becomes a museum."
    narrator "Nobody uses the governance forum anymore. It becomes like a museum: quiet, and full of old things."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:59
translate plain ch3_governance_60dd2b45:

    # narrator "Maya's been quiet for two weeks. Commits at 3 a.m., camera off at standups. You noticed. (Didn't you?)"
    narrator "Maya has been quiet for two weeks. She commits code at 3 a.m. She keeps her camera off at standups."
    narrator "You noticed. (Didn't you?)"

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:61
translate plain ch3_governance_37c16441:

    # m "...A competitor offered me three times my salary. I told them I'd think about it. I've been thinking about it for two weeks."
    m "...A competitor offered me three times my salary."
    m "I told them I'd think about it. I've been thinking about it for two weeks."
    narrator "{color=#578C88}TIP:{/color} You can ask what she needs, call the offer a betrayal, or give her space. Each answer shows her a different kind of leader."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:68
translate plain ch3_governance_0503684e:

    # m "...I need to not be the only adult about infrastructure. And a real vacation. That's it. That's the whole ransom."
    m "...I need to not be the only responsible adult for our infrastructure. Someone else has to care too."
    m "And I need a real vacation. That's it. That's my whole price for staying."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:69
translate plain ch3_governance_ec7e4f81:

    # narrator "She stays. It costs you a hire and two weeks of her silence. Cheap."
    narrator "She stays. It costs you one new hire and two weeks of her silence. That's a cheap price."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:72
translate plain ch3_governance_728dc585:

    # m "I appreciate the words. But you're running on fumes too — you can't refill me from an empty tank."
    m "Thank you for saying that. But you're exhausted too."
    m "You can't give me energy that you don't have."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:73
translate plain ch3_governance_7da5dc93:

    # narrator "She agrees to 'transition slowly.' You both know what that means."
    narrator "She agrees to 'transition slowly.' You both know what that really means: she is leaving."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:81
translate plain ch3_governance_2f609da3:

    # m "A betrayal? [player_name], I deferred salary for eight months. Read the resignation email whenever you're ready."
    m "A betrayal? [player_name], I took deferred salary for eight months. I worked then, and waited to get paid."
    m "My resignation email is waiting for you. Read it whenever you're ready."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:86
translate plain ch3_governance_4c5616eb:

    # narrator "Two weeks of space later: a resignation email, timestamped 3:04 a.m. 'Please have your lawyer contact mine regarding equity separation.'"
    narrator "Two weeks of 'space' later, a resignation email arrives. It was sent at 3:04 a.m."
    narrator "It says: 'Please have your lawyer contact mine regarding equity separation.' She wants to settle what she owns."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:90
translate plain ch3_governance_85889c30:

    # narrator "The vesting agreement does its quiet work: her unvested tokens return to the treasury. Sad, clean, survivable."
    narrator "The vesting agreement does its quiet work. Vesting means she earned her tokens slowly, over time."
    narrator "Her unvested tokens, the part she hadn't earned yet, go back to the treasury. It's sad, but clean. You can survive this."

# game/ch3_governance.rpy:94
translate plain ch3_governance_813d1fa7:

    # narrator "No vesting. She leaves with every token — dead equity on your cap table, a veto-sized ghost in every future vote. The lawyers begin to circle."
    narrator "There was no vesting. So she leaves with every token."
    narrator "That's dead equity on your cap table: a big owner who no longer works here. Like a ghost, her votes will sit in every future decision, big enough to block it."
    narrator "The lawyers start to gather."

